ve a strategic  self interests in supporting legality  verification the illegal producer is in  the timber industry do because they  actually gain an economic benefit by  weeding out supply you end up increasing  the price and that's good for their  bottom line environmental activists see  they need to be support this to actually  weed out some of the worst practices in  the world illegally harvested timber as  a result you have very strange  bedfellows of bootleggers and Baptists  meaning two very different kinds of  groups supporting meeting during these  policies aimed at reducing illegal  logging so once they get in place the  notions of why the coalition's are  emerging and we realize that means  tending simultaneously to environmental  and economic interests then we can think  about the second question which is how  do we do it and that means developing a  system in which companies have an  incentive in being part of the tracking  process so as long as the costs of  compliance that is building tracking  systems of legally harvested timber are  smaller than the economic rents they  achieve by reducing the bad supply  companies will be in favor of helping  promote these collaborative efforts and  this means that only by having companies  government stakeholders across the world  united by these forest products supply  chains together collaborating on  tracking can we actually achieve some  kind of enduring success on the  mechanisms side of this question so help  me understand if someone is doing  illegal logging how that plays into what  you've just laid out okay thank you very  good question because what we're finding  is that illegal activity which can occur  in a number of circumstances but it's  essentially capturing those processes  where companies and individuals go into  forests  and harvest the logs without any  permission I'm doing so without any  kinds of constraints and controls about  environmental stewardship without pay  attention to annual harvest rates and so  on can lead to enduring and lasting  effects of degradation to the  environment because there's no legally  enforced environmental stewardship  taking place so by weeding out this  lumber from markets and requiring that  companies and individuals follow local  law laws about reforestation about how  to harvest in an eco sensitive way we  can start to begin to make a difference  on the ground governing these global  environmental challenges in the forest  sector so can actually lead to a win-win  opportunity where those who do produce  in a legal way may actually help address  these ongoing challenges of  deforestation and degradation versus  being part of the problem  so again though if someone has a  shipment of illegally harvested wood yes  what actually happens no one will buy it  because they know it's illegal what  exactly happens in that instance so  what's happened recently and why this is  a very important topic for students of  international regulation policy and  international trade is that both the  United States and the European Union  have passed international trade  legislation within their own territories  requiring that any importer of wood show  do care that it did not come from  illegal sources so although it's a  market mechanism the compliance  incentives come from US and EU domestic  legislation and this has created a much  more different incentive than Eco  labeling programs which relied on  goodwill on the part of consumers and  retailers to support these things it  turns out that fear of going to jail for  breaking the law is a much greater  motivator than simple goodwill wasn't in  the past  right it's one thing though to have the  UK and the u.s. have this agreement in  looking at the maps it looks like South  America China parts of Russia are really  the ones who are doing most of the  illegal stuff  so are they trading with each other and  are there no ramifications there in a  10-year period from the mid 1990s to the  mid